<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ripudaman Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Maharaja Ripudaman Singh of Nabha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ripudaman Singh was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Nabha, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> princely state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, from 1911 to 1923. He supported Sikh religious reform and Indian nationalist causes, which brought him into conflict with the British. After a dispute with neighbouring Patiala he was forced to abdicate in 1923, prompting the Jaito protest, and he died in exile at Kodaikanal in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ripudaman-singh.jpg" alt="Ripudaman Singh" width="292" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripudhaman_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ripudaman Singh was born at Nabha on 4 March 1883, the only son and heir of Hira Singh. Against British advice his father did not send him to college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> but had him taught in the traditional way by Kahn Singh of Nabha from 1887 to 1893, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture. This is the reason given for his attachment to the Tat Khalsa reading of Sikhism, rather than the Sanatan Sikh school his father followed.</p><p>In 1905, for the Chief Khalsa Diwan, he persuaded the British to ban idol worship on the parkrama of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, which so angered his father that he lost his post as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">vakil</a> of Nabha. Between 1906 and 1908 he sat in the legislative council, where he sponsored the Sikh Anand Marriage Act. He was the main figure behind the Sikh Educational Conferences, first held in 1908, and with Kahn Singh founded the Central Khalsa Diwan in 1909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his father's death in 1911 he took the gadi of Nabha but refused to be crowned by the Viceroy, as was then the custom for a senior prince. He reformed the state judiciary, passed laws for female education and a marriage act, set up a legislature and an executive council, and in 1912 became a judge of the Nabha High Court. A friend of Lala Lajpat Rai and other leaders of the swaraj movement, he refused to send Nabha state forces to the British Indian Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After the Amritsar Massacre of 1919 he openly opposed the British, clashing with his distant cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupinder-singh-of-patiala">Bhupinder Singh of Patiala</a>, a firm supporter of British rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After leaving Nabha in 1923 he settled at Dehradun on a large British allowance, but kept trying to regain some control of the state. In 1927 he made a pilgrimage to Hazur Sahib, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> initiation again and the name Gurcharan Singh. In 1928 the British stripped him of his rank and titles for sedition, recognised his eldest son, Pratap Singh, and exiled him to Kodaikanal in the Madras Presidency, where he was known officially as Sardar Gurcharan Singh. He died there on 12 December 1942, aged 59.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ripudaman Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maharaja <b>Ripudaman Singh</b> backed Sikh reform and Indian nationalist causes and was forced to abdicate in 1923, prompting the Jaito morcha protest by the Akalis.</blockquote><a href="/state/nabha-state">Nabha State</a></li></ul></section>
